Calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction in which 3 moles of carbon monoxide (CO) gas is burned with excess oxygen gas to form 3 moles of carbon dioxide (CO2) gas. The reaction is carried out at constant pressure and a temperature of 298 K.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the standard molar entropies of the reactants and products:

CO(g) + 1/2 O2(g) → CO2(g)

Given the reaction:  
3 CO(g) + 3/2 O2(g) → 3 CO2(g)

First, we need the standard molar entropies (S°) of each substance at 298 K:

- S°(CO) = 170.9 J/mol·K
- S°(O2) = 205.0 J/mol·K
- S°(CO2) = 213.7 J/mol·K

Now, we can use the following formula to calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction:

ΔS = Σn * S°(products) - Σn * S'(reactants)

where n is the number of moles of each substance in the balanced reaction.

For the given reaction (3 CO(g) + 3/2 O2(g) → 3 CO2(g)):

ΔS = 3 * S°(CO2) - [(3 * S°(CO)) + (3/2) * S°(O2)]

ΔS = 3 * (213.7 J/mol·K) - [3 * (170.9 J/mol·K) + (3/2) * (205.0 J/mol·K)]

ΔS = 641.1 J/mol·K - [512.7 J/mol·K + 307.5 J/mol·K]

ΔS = 641.1 J/mol·K - 820.2 J/mol·K

ΔS = -179.1 J/mol·K

The change in entropy for the reaction is -179.1 J/K.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

