To calculate the standard enthalpy change (ΔH°) for the reaction, we can use the following formula:

ΔH° = Σ ΔH°f(products) - Σ ΔH°f(reactants)

where ΔH°f is the standard enthalpy of formation.

For the given reaction:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(s)OH2(s)

The products are Ca(OH)2(s) and the reactants are CaO(s) and H2O(l).

Given the standard enthalpies of formation:

ΔH°f[CaO(s)] = -635.1 kJ/mol
ΔH°f[H2O(l)] = -285.8 kJ/mol
ΔH°f[Ca(OH)2(s)] = -986.2 kJ/mol

Now, we can calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction:

ΔH° = ΔH°f[Ca(OH)2(s)] - (ΔH°f[CaO(s)] + ΔH°f[H2O(l)] )
ΔH° = (-986.2 kJ/mol) - (-635.1 kJ/mol + -285.8 kJ/mol)
ΔH° = -986.2 kJ/mol - (-920.9 kJ/mol)
ΔH° = -986.2 kJ/mol + 920.9 kJ/mol
ΔH° = -65.3 kJ/mol

The standard enthalpy change for the reaction between calcium oxide and water to form calcium hydrolyzed is -65.3 kJ/mol.
