   \(H_2SO_4\) is a strong binary acid, so its normality is equal to its molarity.
   \[
   N = 2 \times \text{Molarity}
   \]
   For \(H_2SO_4\), the equivalent weight is the amount of \(H_2SO_4\) that provides 2 moles of \(H^+\) ions. Since \(H_2SO_4\) has two \(H^+\) ions, \(N = 2M\).

2. **Moles of \(H_2SO_4\) in the initial solution**:
   \[
   \text{Moles} = \text{Molarity} \times \text{Volume}
   \]
   \[
   \text{Moles in 10.0 L of 2.00 N solution} = 2.00 \text{ N} \times 10.0 \text{ L} = 20.0 \text{ equivalents} = 10.0 \text{ moles}
   \]

3. **Moles of \(H_2SO_4\) in the added solution**:
   Let \(V\) be the volume of 6.00 M \(H_2SO_4\) to be added.
