- \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) is a gas with the following reaction:
   \[
   \text{M}^{2+} + \text{H}_2\text{S} \rightleftharpoons \text{MS} + 2\text{H}^+
   \]
   where \( \text{M}^{2+} \) is the metal ion.

- The solubility of a metal sulfide is determined by its \( K_{sp} \). The lower the \( K_{sp} \), the more insoluble the sulfide. The \( K_{Sp} \) values for the sulfides are:
   - \( \text{Cu}_2\text{S} \): \( K_{sp} = 1.3 \times 10^{-36} \) (very insoluble)
   - \( \text{HgS} \): \( K_{sp} = 1.5 \times 10^{-40} \) (even more insoluble than \( \text{Cu}_2\text{S} \))
   - \( \text{MnS} \): \( K_{sp} = 2.3 \times 10^{-11} \) (incompressible)
   - \( \text{NiS} \): \( K_{sp} = 1.4 \times 10^{-12} \) (incompressible)

- **Answer**:
   - \( \text{Cu}^2+ \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) form \( \text{CuS} \) and \( \text{HgS} \), respectively, which are very insoluble.
   - \( \text{Mn}^{2+} \) and \( \text{Ni}^{2+} \) form \( \text{MnS} \) and \( \text{NiS \), respectively, which are less insoluble.

