<USER_REQUEST>
In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
A σ molecular orbital is characterized by electron density concentrated along the internuclear axis, resulting from head-on overlap of atomic orbitals. In contrast, a π molecular orbital has electron density concentrated on either side of the internuclear axis, arising from sideways overlap of atomic orbitals. This difference in electron density distribution affects the type of bonding: σ bonds are generally stronger and more stable, while π bonds are weaker and more reactive, often involved in double and triple bonds.
</GROUND_TRUTH>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The key difference between σ (sigma) and π (pi) molecular orbitals in terms of electron density distribution lies in their symmetry with respect to the interatomic nucleus.  

The difference is:
- **σ (Sigma) Molecular Orbitals**: These have symmetrical distribution of electron density around the nuclear plane (the line connecting the two atomic nuclei). This means if you rotate the molecule along the bond axis, the electron density distribution remains unchanged.  
- **π (Pi) Molecular Orbitals**: These have a wave-like distribution of electron density above and below the nuclear plane.  
- If you rotate the molecule along the bond axis, the electron density distribution is inverted (e.g., the electron density that was above the nucleus is now below).  

Thus, the electron density in a π orbital is not symmetric with respect to the nuclear axis.  

\boxed{\text{σ orbitals are symmetric, while π orbitals are antisymmetric with respect to the interatomic nucleus.}}
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

