","To solve this problem, we use the relative speeds of the jogher and the train.
The speed of the jogerer is 9 km/hr, and the speed of the train is 45 km/hr. Since both are moving in the same direction, the relative speed of the train with respect to the jogher is:
\[ \text{Relative speed} = 45 \text{ km/hr} - 9 \text{ km/hr} = 36 \text{ km/hr} \]
First, convert the relative speed to meters per second:
\[ 36 \text{ km/hr} = 36 \times \frac{1000}{3600} \text{ m/s} = 36 \times \frac{10}{36} \text{ m/s} = 10 \text{ m/s} \]
The jogher is initially 150 meters ahead of the train's edge. The time taken for the train to pass the jugher is 25 seconds. During this time, the train must cover the initial distance between the jogher and the back of the train (150 meters), and the length of the train. Let the length of the train be $L$ meters. The total distance the train must cover is $150 + L$ meters.
Using the formula:
\[ \text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} \]
\[ 150 + L = 10 \times 25 \]
\[ 150 + L = 250 \]
\[ L = 250 - 150 \]
\[ L = 100 \text{ meters} \]
Thus, the length of the train is 100 meters.

