","To prove the statement \(a \equiv b \pmod{10} \iff (a \equiv b \equiv b \pmod{2} \text{ and } a \equiv b \pmod{5})\), we proceed in two parts:

1. **(Implication 1):** Suppose \(a \equiv b \pmod{10}\). This means that \(10 \mid (a - b)\), so \(a - b = 10k\) for some integer \(k\).

   Since \(10 = 2 \cdot 5\), it implies that \(2 \mid (a - b)\) and \(5 \mid (a - b)\). Thus, \(a - b = 2m\) for some integer \(m\) and \(a - b = 5n\) for some integer \(n\).

   Thus, \(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\) and \(a \equiv b \pmod{5}\).

2. **(Implacement 2):** Suppose \(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\) and \(a \equiv b \pmod{5}\). This means that \(2 \mid (a - b)\) and \(5 \mid (a - b)\). Thus, \(a - b = 2m\) and \(a - b = 5n\) for integers \(m\) and \(n\).

