","To calculate the possible reaction times, we first need to convert the flow rate to volume per unit time (V) and the tube diameter to radius (r) for calculations. We can then use these values to calculate the velocity (v) of the fluid in the tube and the separation distance (z) between the separation point and the top of the tube.

1. **Convert flow rate to cm³/s:**
   \[
   \text{Flow rate} = 10 \, \text{cm}^3/\text{min} = 10 \, \text{cm}^3/(60 \, \text{s}) = 0.167 \, \text{cm}^3/\text{s}
   \]

2. **Calculate the cross-sectional area of the tube:**
   \[
   \text{Diameter} = 0.1 \, \text{mm} = 0.01 \, \text{cm}
   \]
   \[
   \text{Radius} = 0.005 \, \text{cm}
   \]
   \[
   \text{Cross-sectional area} = \pi r^2 = \pi (0.005 \, \text{cm})^2 = 7.85 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{cm}^2
   \]

3. **Calculate the velocity of the fluid in the tube:**
   \[
   \text{Velocity} = \frac{\text{Flow rate}}{\text{Cross-sectional area}} = \frac{0.167 \, \text{cm}^3/\text{s}}{7.85 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{cm}^2} = 2.12 \, \text{cm/s}
   \]

4. **Calculate the separation distance (z) for the given tube lengths (1-10 cm):**
   \[
   \text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Velocity}}
   \]

   For a tube length of 1 cm:
   \[
   t = \frac{1 \, \text{cm}}{2.12 \, \text{cm/s}} = 0.472 \, \text{s}
   \]

   For a tube length of 10 cm:
   \[
   t = \frac{10 \, \text{cm}}{2.12 \, \text{cm/s}} = 4.72 \, \text{s}
   \]

