The net chemical reaction during the discharging of a cell in a gold-platinum electrodes cell is:  
\[\text{Pb(s)} + \text{Ag}_2\text{SO}_4(s) + \text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \rightleftharpoons \text{PbSO}_4(s) + 2\text{Ag(s)} \]  
The cell potential is \(\boxed{1.54 \text{ V}}\).  

For a typical lead-acid device, the reaction is:  
\[\text{Pb(s)} + \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4(aq) \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4(s) + 2\text{H}^+(aq) \]  
At the negative electrode (electrode \(\text{Ag}\)):  
\[\text{Ag}^+ + \text{e}^- \rightarrow \text{Ag} \quad E^\circ = +0.80 \text{ V} \]  
At the positive electrode (electrode \(\text{Pb}\)):  
\[\text{PbO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O} + \text{H}^+ + \text{e}^-} \rightarrow \text{PbO} + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \quad E^\circ = +1.69 \text{V} \]  
The overall reaction is:  
\[\text{PbO}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 + 2\text{H}^+ + 2\text{e}^- \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \]  

Thus, the overall reaction is:  
\[\text{Pb} + \text{PbO}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow \text{2PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \]  

The correct answer is:  
\[\text{Pb(s)} + \text{PbO}_2(s) + 2\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4(aq) \rightarrow \text{2PbSO}_4(s) + 2\text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \]
