","To calculate the work done by the gas during the isothermal expansion, we use the formula for work done in an isothermal process:

\[
W = -nRT \ln \left( \frac{V_2}{V_1} \right)
\]

where:
- \( W = \text{work done} \)
- \( n = \text{number of moles} \)
- \( R = 8.314 \text{ J/mol·K} = 0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K} \) (using L-atm as the work unit)
- \( T = \text{temperature} \)
- \( V_1 = \text{initial volume} \)
- \( V_2 = \text{final volume} \)

First, we need to find the temperature \( T \). We can use the ideal gas law for the initial state:

\[
PV = nRT
\]

Substitute the given values:
\[
(2 \text{ atm})(4 \text{ L}) = (2 \text{ mol})(0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K})(T)
\]

Solve for \( T \):
\[
T = \frac{8}{2 \times 0.0821} = \frac{8}{0.1642} \approx 48.8 \text{ K}
\]

Now, we can calculate the work done:

\[
W = -nRT \ln \left( \frac{V_2}{V_1} \right) = -(2 \text{ mol})(0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K})(48.8 \text{ K}) \ln \left( \frac{8 \text{ L}}{4 \text{ L}} \right)
\]

\[
W = -(2 \times 0.0821 \times 48.8) \ln(2) = -0.805 \ln(2)
\]

\[
W \approx -0.805 \times 0.693 \approx -0.559 \text{ L·atm}
\]

Convert to joules:
\[
W = -0.559 \text{ L·atm} \times 101.325 \text{ J/L·atm} \approx -56.6 \text{ J}
\]

Thus, the work done by the gas during the expansion process is \(\boxed{-0.559 \text{ L·atm}\) or \(\boxed{-56.6 \text{ J}}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
